WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.302 --> 00:00:06.451
That evening I asked Herbert what he did for a living, and he replied, "I'm an

2
00:00:06.452 --> 00:00:14.459
insurer of ships." I had grand ideas of the wealth and importance of such people, so I

3
00:00:14.460 --> 00:00:20.436
couldn't believe that I'd fought with this pale young gentleman and defeated him. "I will not

4
00:00:20.437 --> 00:00:27.999
be satisfied merely with insuring ships," Herbert explained, leaning back in his chair. "I'll trade in

5
00:00:28.000 --> 00:00:30.166
the East Indies for silks, shawls, spices, and precious woods. That's an interesting trade." "And are the

6
00:00:30.167 --> 00:00:36.174
profits large?" "Tremendous. I'll also trade in the West Indies for sugar, tobacco, and rum." "You'll

7
00:00:36.175 --> 00:00:39.071
want many ships," I said. "An entire fleet." Quite impressed by the magnificence of his business,

8
00:00:39.072 --> 00:00:41.087
I asked him where the ships he insured now were trading. "Oh, I haven't begun insuring ships

9
00:00:41.088 --> 00:00:47.254
yet," he replied. "I work in a counting house." So all of Herbert's profits from the

10
00:00:47.255 --> 00:00:54.095
shipping business were somewhere in the future, I realized. Looking around his rooms, I saw that

11
00:00:54.096 --> 00:00:59.412
he owned nothing but the simplest necessities. Yet he was cheerful and optimistic. We went out

12
00:00:59.413 --> 00:01:07.229
for a walk and then to the theater. The next day we went to church at Westminster

13
00:01:07.230 --> 00:01:15.817
Abbey, and in the afternoon we walked in the parks. I wondered who the blacksmith was

14
00:01:15.818 --> 00:01:20.650
for all the horses there, and I wished it were Joe. It seemed many months since

15
00:01:20.651 --> 00:01:26.213
I had left Joe and Biddy, though it had only been a day. On Monday I went

16
00:01:26.214 --> 00:01:30.229
with Herbert to his counting house for a few hours. Then we took a coach out

17
00:01:30.230 --> 00:01:34.801
to Hammersmith to meet his father, who would be my tutor. Mr. Matthew Pocket took me

18
00:01:34.802 --> 00:01:40.373
into the house and showed me my room, which was a pleasant one. Then he knocked on

19
00:01:40.374 --> 00:01:47.451
the doors of two other rooms and introduced me to two more of his students, Bentley

20
00:01:47.452 --> 00:01:56.285
Drummle and Startop. After two or three days, when I had established myself in my room

21
00:01:56.286 --> 00:02:06.517
and gone back and forth to London several times, Mr. Pocket and I had a long talk

22
00:02:06.518 --> 00:02:13.183
together. It occurred to me that I might be happier if I could stay in London

23
00:02:13.184 --> 00:02:19.255
with Herbert. Mr. Pocket did not object but urged me to get Mr. Jaggers' approval for

24
00:02:19.256 --> 00:02:24.247
this arrangement, which I did. "If I could buy some furniture," I said to Mr. Jaggers, "and

25
00:02:24.248 --> 00:02:25.755
one or two other things, I should be quite at home there." He approved the arrangement

26
00:02:25.756 --> 00:02:31.096
and sent me to see his clerk, John Wemmick, for twenty pounds from my account. "Have

27
00:02:31.097 --> 00:02:33.255
you dined with Mr. Jaggers yet?" asked Wemmick as he counted out the money. "Not yet." "I'll

28
00:02:33.256 --> 00:02:37.509
tell you something. When you dine with him, look at his housekeeper." "Will I see something

29
00:02:37.510 --> 00:02:42.398
unusual?" "You'll see a wild beast tamed. Keep your eye on her." I told him I

30
00:02:42.399 --> 00:02:48.311
would do so, and now I was very curious to dine with Mr. Jaggers. In the

31
00:02:48.312 --> 00:02:54.390
meantime I rowed every evening on the river with Drummle and Startop, each of us in our

32
00:02:54.391 --> 00:03:02.382
own boat. Drummle was idle, haughty, and suspicious. So it was natural that I preferred Startop.

33
00:03:02.383 --> 00:03:08.977
Even in our earliest outings, Startop and I would row near each other, conversing from boat

34
00:03:08.978 --> 00:03:15.787
to boat. Drummle rowed along behind us. But Herbert was my closest friend. I let him use

35
00:03:15.788 --> 00:03:22.461
my boat, which meant he often came down to Hammersmith. And my sharing of his chambers

36
00:03:22.462 --> 00:03:27.279
often took me up to London. We walked between the two places at all hours. I

37
00:03:27.280 --> 00:03:33.858
soon acquired expensive habits, and within a few short months, I began to spend a fabulous amount

38
00:03:33.859 --> 00:03:44.350
of money. But still I stuck to my books and applied myself to my education. At

39
00:03:44.351 --> 00:03:45.874
last Mr. Jaggers invited me to dine. He also included Herbert, Drummle, and Startop. When we

40
00:03:45.875 --> 00:03:48.104
arrived at his house, Mr. Jaggers put his large hand on my shoulder and moved me away

41
00:03:48.105 --> 00:03:49.938
from the others. "Pip, I don't know these people. Who's the spider?" "The spider?" "The blotchy,

42
00:03:49.939 --> 00:03:55.335
sulky fellow." "That's Bentley Drummle." "I like the look of him," said Mr. Jaggers, and he

43
00:03:55.336 --> 00:04:00.438
immediately went over to talk to Drummle. The first floor of the house consisted of a series

44
00:04:00.439 --> 00:04:07.351
of gloomy dark-brown rooms. We took our dinner in the best of these rooms. When we

45
00:04:07.352 --> 00:04:14.335
sat down at the table, the housekeeper began to serve the fish. She was a woman

46
00:04:14.336 --> 00:04:18.827
of about forty, I suppose. She was rather tall and extremely pale, with large eyes. Remembering

47
00:04:18.828 --> 00:04:24.835
Wemmick's remark about her, I watched her carefully. Dinner went well, and when we reached the cheese

48
00:04:24.836 --> 00:04:29.494
course, our conversation turned to our rowing feats. Drummle was teased for always coming up behind

49
00:04:29.495 --> 00:04:33.406
us in that slow way of his. Drummle then said he was more than our master

50
00:04:33.407 --> 00:04:39.557
when it came to rowing. Mr. Jaggers encouraged this talk, and Drummle soon bared his arm to

51
00:04:39.558 --> 00:04:44.303
show how muscular it was. And then we all bared our arms and showed off our

52
00:04:44.304 --> 00:04:47.723
muscles in a ridiculous manner. The housekeeper was at that moment clearing the table. Mr. Jaggers

53
00:04:47.724 --> 00:04:53.716
clapped his large hand like a trap over her hand as she stretched it across the table.

54
00:04:53.717 --> 00:04:57.811
He did this so suddenly that we all stopped our foolish competition. " I'll show you

55
00:04:57.812 --> 00:05:03.382
strength," he said. "Molly, let them see your wrist." Her trapped hand was on the table,

56
00:05:03.383 --> 00:05:09.113
but she had already put her other hand behind her back. "Master, don't," she said in a

57
00:05:09.114 --> 00:05:14.025
low voice with her eyes fixed upon him. "Molly, let them see both your wrists," said

58
00:05:14.026 --> 00:05:20.938
Mr. Jaggers, not looking at her. "Show them. Come!" He took his hand from hers and

59
00:05:20.939 --> 00:05:24.859
turned her wrist up on the table. She brought her other hand from behind her back. That

60
00:05:24.860 --> 00:05:31.938
wrist was very disfigured with deep scars running across it. When she held her hands out,

61
00:05:31.939 --> 00:05:37.446
she took her eyes from Mr. Jaggers and looked at each one of us in turn.

62
00:05:37.447 --> 00:05:42.668
"There's power here," said Mr. Jaggers, coolly tracing the sinews with his finger. "It's remarkable what a

63
00:05:42.669 --> 00:05:52.939
forceful grip is in these hands. I've had occasion to notice many hands, but I've never

64
00:05:52.940 --> 00:05:59.170
seen any stronger—man's or woman's—than these." She continued to look at all of us until he

65
00:05:59.171 --> 00:06:04.884
said, "That'll do, Molly. You can go." After that we ate too much and talked too

66
00:06:04.885 --> 00:06:09.979
much. Drummle argued with all of us. Startop tried to end one argument with some small pleasantry

67
00:06:09.980 --> 00:06:17.892
that made us all laugh. Resenting this, Drummle without any warning picked up a large glass

68
00:06:17.893 --> 00:06:22.408
and would have flung it at Startop's head if Mr. Jaggers had not seized it. "Gentlemen,"

69
00:06:22.409 --> 00:06:26.447
said Mr. Jaggers, deliberately putting down the glass. "I am exceedingly sorry to announce that it is

70
00:06:26.448 --> 00:06:33.241
time for you to go." As we took our leave, I apologized to Mr. Jaggers for

71
00:06:33.242 --> 00:06:36.145
the arguments, and he told me again how much he liked Drummle. "I'm glad you like

72
00:06:36.146 --> 00:06:41.201
him, sir, but I don't." "Then don't have much to do with him, Pip. Avoid him as

73
00:06:41.202 --> 00:06:46.131
often as you can." A month later Drummle left Mr. Pocket's, and I felt great relief.

